WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.450
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.880
MODERATOR: I am pleased to
welcome to the platform William

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.860
J. Clinton, President
of the United States;

00:00:18.860 --> 00:00:24.510
Dr. David D. Ho, graduate of the
Harvard MIT division of Health

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.950
Science and Technology,
professor at Rockefeller

00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:33.720
University, scientific director
and chief executive officer

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:37.290
of the Aaron Diamond
AIDS Research Center

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:42.000
and 1996 Time magazine
Man of the Year;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.030
and the Honorable
Francis J. Duehay, mayor

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.010
of the city of Cambridge.

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.045
Dr. Ho will now
give his address.

00:00:53.045 --> 00:00:55.470
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:05.655 --> 00:01:08.165
HO: Thank you.

00:01:08.165 --> 00:01:10.580
It's great to be back.

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:16.410
Good morning, President Clinton,
Mayor Duehay, President Vest,

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:22.400
Mr. D'Arbeloff, members of the
MIT Corporation and faculty.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:25.730
To the graduates and their
families and friends,

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:28.400
my heartfelt congratulations--

00:01:28.400 --> 00:01:33.830
it is indeed a momentous
day in your lives.

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:37.880
What an incredible honor it
is for me to return to MIT

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:40.340
as a commencement speaker.

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:44.630
As a young immigrant boy growing
up in Southern California,

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:49.850
I was awed by this august
institution from afar.

00:01:49.850 --> 00:01:53.840
My love for science was
inspired by the genius

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:58.520
of extraordinary men like
Feynmann, Luria, Baltimore,

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.540
and Ting, whose presence,
at one time or another,

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:05.330
graced this campus.

00:02:05.330 --> 00:02:09.470
Later, it was within the
Harvard MIT Health Sciences

00:02:09.470 --> 00:02:14.720
and Technology program where
my clinical skills were honed,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.680
where my scientific
interests were solidified,

00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.340
and where I truly learned
to tackle research

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.480
with a multi-disciplinary
approach, not

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.150
limited by arbitrary
boundaries that

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:31.140
separate biomedical sciences
from physics, chemistry,

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.160
engineering, and mathematics.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:38.400
I will forever be
indebted to you.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.350
I'm here today because I've been
given a great deal of praise

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:48.770
and recognition for recent
advances made in AIDS research.

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:53.000
Achievements in research seldom
belong to a single individual.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:56.030
Science is a richly
collaborative endeavor

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.270
and my personal recognition
is merely symbolic

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:01.200
for the many
important discoveries

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:05.510
and contributions made by a
cadre of talented scientists

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.610
in the field.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:11.840
As Newton aptly put it in
a letter to Robert Hook,

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:14.660
"If I had seen further,
it is by standing

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:18.590
upon the shoulder of giants."

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:22.250
The recent media attention
focused on age research

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:25.190
has provided a
unique opportunity

00:03:25.190 --> 00:03:29.540
to educate the public
at large about a plague

00:03:29.540 --> 00:03:34.100
of staggering dimensions and
to advocate to our leader

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.250
a proper course of action.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.780
It is also gratifying
to see that the media is

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.490
willing, on occasions,
to prominently feature

00:03:43.490 --> 00:03:49.050
scientists with accomplishments
that move our society forward.

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:52.940
However, on a personal
note, I find the spotlight

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:54.790
is hot enough to bake.

00:03:54.790 --> 00:03:58.580
And as scientists-- as
Einstein had cautioned,

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.180
"The only way to avoid
the corruption of praise

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.690
is to keep on working."

00:04:05.690 --> 00:04:09.380
I feel extremely
privileged to work on AIDS.

00:04:09.380 --> 00:04:13.580
As a young physician
in Los Angeles in 1981,

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:16.130
I was fortunate
enough to witness

00:04:16.130 --> 00:04:20.149
the beginning of the visible
part of the epidemic.

00:04:20.149 --> 00:04:25.490
Over the course of a year,
young men, one after another,

00:04:25.490 --> 00:04:29.660
presented to the hospital with
a multitude of infections,

00:04:29.660 --> 00:04:33.650
leading to death
within days to weeks.

00:04:33.650 --> 00:04:37.970
It was evident that their
immune system was damaged.

00:04:37.970 --> 00:04:40.430
But by what?

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.210
Their medical history strongly
suggested the possibility

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:50.660
of a sexually transmitted agent
that caused immunodeficiency.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:54.380
And yet, any description
of a similar syndrome

00:04:54.380 --> 00:04:58.820
was nowhere to be found
in the medical literature.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:02.090
The disease was obviously new.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:06.380
In this manner, AIDS appeared
insidiously and mystified

00:05:06.380 --> 00:05:09.770
doctors and scientists alike.

00:05:09.770 --> 00:05:14.150
No one could have predicted
that 17 years later we

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:17.960
would face a global
epidemic of HIV infection

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:23.240
that is arguably the
plague of the Millennium.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:27.620
Today, HIV continues to
spread at an alarming rate

00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:32.420
of 16,000 new cases per day.

00:05:32.420 --> 00:05:35.390
And more than 50
million infections

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:39.320
are expected by the year 2000.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:43.700
For a biomedical scientist,
what could represent a greater

00:05:43.700 --> 00:05:46.970
opportunity than
to conduct research

00:05:46.970 --> 00:05:49.460
on a lethal microbe
that threatens

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.000
the health of the entire world?

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:57.950
Members of the class of
'98, as you move on in life,

00:05:57.950 --> 00:06:02.130
be prepared to take advantage
of the opportunities that

00:06:02.130 --> 00:06:04.850
have bubbled up by serendipity.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:07.400
Then have the courage
and conviction

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:09.535
in pursuing your
goals and ideals.

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.550
In science and engineering, you
have chosen noble professions,

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:20.790
ones filled with excitement.

00:06:20.790 --> 00:06:24.360
Nothing is more thrilling
to me than the process

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.730
of scientific discovery.

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:30.240
When the wonder of
nature is revealed,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:34.530
one is left breathless
and awestruck.

00:06:34.530 --> 00:06:38.130
Imagine the joy and
intellectual satisfaction

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:42.000
when the constant expansion
of our vast universe

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.740
was discovered, when the complex
nature of the chemical bond

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:52.020
was understood, or when the
double helical structure of DNA

00:06:52.020 --> 00:06:53.610
was solved.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:58.260
Imagine the excitement that must
have pervaded this campus when

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:04.770
the synthesis of DNA was
achieved from an RNA template.

00:07:04.770 --> 00:07:08.820
Imagine, as well, the
fruits of science,

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:12.750
the sense of accomplishment
in those engineers

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:16.650
when an electrical current was
transformed into light, when

00:07:16.650 --> 00:07:20.160
sound waves were transmitted
across long distances

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:23.940
in a wire, or when
a cushion of air

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:28.860
was harvested to send
men soaring into the sky.

00:07:28.860 --> 00:07:34.380
Allow me a more modest,
but personal, anecdote.

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:37.080
Beginning in '91,
my colleagues and I

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.010
had the privilege of working
with structural biologists

00:07:41.010 --> 00:07:44.910
and medicinal chemists to
test small chemicals that

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:49.230
might intercalate into the
catalytic site of the HIV

00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:53.550
protease, an enzyme
essential for the production

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:56.280
of infectious progeny virus.

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.350
So overwhelming
was the excitement

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:02.940
that overtook us when substances
were found to potently inhibit

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.980
this enzyme, thereby blocking
viral replication in the test

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:09.500
tube.

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:13.350
Three years later, we
again had the privilege

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:17.130
of being the first to administer
one of these chemicals

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:19.170
to infected patients.

00:08:19.170 --> 00:08:23.070
Unmatched were the
joy and amazement

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:29.220
as we watched the level of
HIV fall ever so dramatically.

00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:31.290
At first, little
did we know that we

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:35.730
were sitting on top of a
fundamental discovery in AIDS

00:08:35.730 --> 00:08:36.840
research.

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.480
But shortly
thereafter, by simply

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.750
asking why does the virus
fall, and why does it

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:46.500
fall in that manner,
it quickly dawned on us

00:08:46.500 --> 00:08:49.680
that HIV must be
turning over rapidly

00:08:49.680 --> 00:08:53.940
in a very dynamic
equilibrium with a host.

00:08:53.940 --> 00:08:57.960
Using data from patients,
working with mathematicians,

00:08:57.960 --> 00:09:02.490
we proved HIV replication
in the patient

00:09:02.490 --> 00:09:05.790
was rapid and remorseless.

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:08.580
In the course of
only a few weeks,

00:09:08.580 --> 00:09:13.620
the old paradigm that HIV
was largely a latent virus

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:15.930
was completely shattered.

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:20.780
So incredible was the ensuing
intellectual satisfaction

00:09:20.780 --> 00:09:25.170
that I now fully appreciate
the meaning of a line

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.420
in a book, The Ascendant Man.

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:30.600
It reads, "When the
answers are simple,

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:35.420
then you hear God thinking."

00:09:35.420 --> 00:09:39.410
Despite the breakneck speed
of scientific discovery

00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.020
in the field, AIDS
patients already

00:09:42.020 --> 00:09:47.020
faced a decade and a half of
horror and disappointment.

00:09:47.020 --> 00:09:50.870
But because of
science there is hope.

00:09:50.870 --> 00:09:54.510
In the past two years, with
new knowledge and new therapy,

00:09:54.510 --> 00:09:57.770
it has become possible
to control HIV

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:00.320
so effectively that
the virus is no

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:03.590
longer detectable in
the infected person.

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:06.080
This dramatic
attack is associated

00:10:06.080 --> 00:10:10.010
with a substantial clinical
benefit to the patient.

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:13.400
For the first time in
this dreadful epidemic,

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:17.360
the tide has begun to
turn against the virus.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:20.870
Although a cure is
still not in hand,

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:24.860
as stated in Time
magazine, the worst fear,

00:10:24.860 --> 00:10:28.370
the one that seeded a
decade with this fear,

00:10:28.370 --> 00:10:33.050
the foreboding sense that the
AIDS virus might be invincible

00:10:33.050 --> 00:10:35.720
has finally been subdued.

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:39.590
After years of cursing
the darkness of AIDS,

00:10:39.590 --> 00:10:45.200
a candle of hope has
been lit by science.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:48.390
AIDS, however, is not over.

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:53.840
Worldwide, most infected persons
cannot access the promising new

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:58.790
therapies and much remains to be
done in controlling the spread

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:00.320
of this epidemic.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:04.850
It is my deepest hope that the
recent scientific advances will

00:11:04.850 --> 00:11:09.200
inspire government, academia,
and the private sector

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.160
to remain vigilant and to
redouble our efforts to bring

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:15.620
an end to this tragedy.

00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:18.440
Prevention is the ultimate
key to controlling

00:11:18.440 --> 00:11:24.320
the epidemic and vaccine science
must now take center stage,

00:11:24.320 --> 00:11:27.440
as was declared by
President Clinton last year.

00:11:30.530 --> 00:11:32.900
Recently my thoughts
had taken me

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:36.560
on a number of
self-reflective journeys.

00:11:36.560 --> 00:11:40.700
Let me humbly share a
few with the graduates.

00:11:40.700 --> 00:11:44.210
In our experiments
attempting to eliminate HIV

00:11:44.210 --> 00:11:46.820
from an infected
person, I've learned

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:51.560
that success in research, as
is the case in most endeavors,

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:55.340
requires bold decision
making and a willingness

00:11:55.340 --> 00:11:57.810
to take informed risks.

00:11:57.810 --> 00:12:02.000
As so eloquently stated by
Harrow Shapiro of Princeton,

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:07.390
"An excessive zeal to avoid
all risks is, in the end,

00:12:07.390 --> 00:12:14.020
an acceptance of mediocrity and
an abdication of leadership."

00:12:14.020 --> 00:12:17.390
Graduates, you must take
on the toughest challenge,

00:12:17.390 --> 00:12:21.560
but view it as the
greatest opportunity.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:28.040
For every noble work is, at
first, seemingly impossible.

00:12:28.040 --> 00:12:30.290
Heed the wise words
of Teddy Roosevelt

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:33.860
before the turn of the
century, "Far better it

00:12:33.860 --> 00:12:37.910
is to dare mighty things, to
win glorious triumphs even

00:12:37.910 --> 00:12:40.550
though checkered by
failure, than to rank

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:45.110
with those poor spirits who
neither enjoy nor suffer much,

00:12:45.110 --> 00:12:48.350
because they live in the
great twilight that knows

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:52.760
neither victory nor defeat."

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:55.310
More and more I've also
reflected on the role

00:12:55.310 --> 00:12:57.990
of science in our society.

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:00.140
I'm disturbed by the
public's indifference

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:03.260
to what we do as
scientists and engineers.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:08.750
As children, we all began
with a real zest for science.

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:13.160
We were curious and provocative,
and insightful questions

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.260
blurted out constantly.

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:20.120
However, by early adulthood,
this proclivity to science

00:13:20.120 --> 00:13:22.460
and the joy of
discovery have somehow

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:28.700
dissipated in most, only to
be replaced by science phobia.

00:13:28.700 --> 00:13:31.610
Consequently, it
is not surprising

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:36.380
that our society has often
stereotyped, unflatteringly,

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:39.550
those scientists and
engineers who carry out

00:13:39.550 --> 00:13:43.880
incomprehensible lines of work.

00:13:43.880 --> 00:13:46.280
Edison, Bell, and
the Wright brothers

00:13:46.280 --> 00:13:50.660
would be today's nerds,
even though the products

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:54.770
of their ingenuity have
made our world better.

00:13:54.770 --> 00:13:56.720
I'm particularly
saddened by the fact

00:13:56.720 --> 00:13:59.700
that many of my personal
heroes in science

00:13:59.700 --> 00:14:04.160
are virtual unknowns
to a typical citizen.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:07.340
While certain dubious
characters are universally

00:14:07.340 --> 00:14:12.140
recognized and often
glorified by our society.

00:14:12.140 --> 00:14:16.160
As scientists and engineers,
we must change this

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:19.790
by learning to present our
work in both interesting and

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:21.680
understandable ways.

00:14:21.680 --> 00:14:25.610
Indeed, it is our
responsibility to bring back

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:29.780
that spark, the sense
of wonder about nature,

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.720
that lies within every citizen.

00:14:35.320 --> 00:14:39.770
I'm equally troubled by this
society's lack of commitment

00:14:39.770 --> 00:14:43.220
to research in basic science.

00:14:43.220 --> 00:14:46.640
A former president said
in a campaign speech,

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:51.680
why should we subsidize
intellectual curiosity?

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.960
The simple, but
naive question speaks

00:14:53.960 --> 00:14:57.380
volumes of the lack
of appreciation

00:14:57.380 --> 00:15:02.120
of the richness that uninhibited
fundamental research has

00:15:02.120 --> 00:15:03.950
brought us.

00:15:03.950 --> 00:15:08.810
Carl Sagan said it best when he
wrote, "Maxwell wasn't thinking

00:15:08.810 --> 00:15:12.470
of radio, radar, and television
when he first scratched out

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:16.760
the fundamental equations
of electromagnetism.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:20.600
Newton wasn't dreaming of
space flights or communications

00:15:20.600 --> 00:15:23.690
satellites when he
first understood

00:15:23.690 --> 00:15:25.790
the motion of the moon.

00:15:25.790 --> 00:15:29.450
Roentgen wasn't contemplating
medical diagnosis

00:15:29.450 --> 00:15:32.600
when he investigated a
penetrating radiation so

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.880
mysterious he called it x-rays.

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:38.930
Curie wasn't thinking
of cancer therapy

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:43.170
when she painstakingly extracted
minute amounts of radium

00:15:43.170 --> 00:15:46.010
from tons of pitchblende.

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:49.640
Fleming wasn't planning on
saving lives of millions

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.230
with antibiotics when he noticed
a circle free of bacteria

00:15:54.230 --> 00:15:57.020
around a growth of mold.

00:15:57.020 --> 00:15:59.720
Watson and Crick
weren't imagining

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.970
the cure of genetic
diseases when

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:07.970
they puzzled over the X-ray
diffraction pattern of DNA."

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:10.460
These discoveries
and a multitude

00:16:10.460 --> 00:16:13.190
of others that graced
and characterized

00:16:13.190 --> 00:16:17.630
our time were made,
ultimately, by scientists given

00:16:17.630 --> 00:16:21.680
the opportunity to explore
what in their opinions

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.370
were basic questions and nature.

00:16:25.370 --> 00:16:29.810
Cutting off fundamental,
curiosity driven science

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:33.620
is like eating the seed corn.

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:37.350
George Washington
said in 1790, "there

00:16:37.350 --> 00:16:41.080
is nothing which can better
deserve our patronage

00:16:41.080 --> 00:16:43.760
than the promotion of science.

00:16:43.760 --> 00:16:48.110
Knowledge is, in every
country, the surest basis

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:50.540
of public happiness."

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:54.530
Graduates, in the
best tradition of MIT,

00:16:54.530 --> 00:16:59.540
always maintain unwaveringly a
deep commitment to excellence.

00:16:59.540 --> 00:17:02.570
As you enter a career in
science or engineering,

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:06.319
stay in the forefront
of your chosen field,

00:17:06.319 --> 00:17:10.790
and never permit the excellence
of your work to be compromised.

00:17:10.790 --> 00:17:13.280
Believe in what you are doing.

00:17:13.280 --> 00:17:17.180
As Max Planck said,
"Over the entrance

00:17:17.180 --> 00:17:22.410
of the gates to the temple of
science are written the words,

00:17:22.410 --> 00:17:23.920
ye must have faith.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:27.859
It is a quality which the
scientists cannot dispense

00:17:27.859 --> 00:17:30.480
with."

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:33.060
I remember with a
great deal of fondness

00:17:33.060 --> 00:17:36.420
my mates from the class of '74.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:41.010
Just like you, what
a bunch they were.

00:17:41.010 --> 00:17:45.780
Stay young at heart, but
should you need to grow,

00:17:45.780 --> 00:17:48.390
do not suppress
your individuality

00:17:48.390 --> 00:17:51.690
and insist on conformity.

00:17:51.690 --> 00:17:55.620
"Continue to let
imagination and creativity

00:17:55.620 --> 00:18:01.050
percolate throughout your
lives, for every great advance

00:18:01.050 --> 00:18:07.080
in science has issued from the
new audacity of imagination,"

00:18:07.080 --> 00:18:10.450
said John Dewey.

00:18:10.450 --> 00:18:15.900
I also reflected on the
contribution of my heritage

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:17.810
to my career.

00:18:17.810 --> 00:18:20.430
Were it not for the
profound Asian respect

00:18:20.430 --> 00:18:24.960
for intellectual achievements
and scholarly endeavors,

00:18:24.960 --> 00:18:28.520
a scientist I
might not be today.

00:18:28.520 --> 00:18:32.310
Moreover, values of
drive, dedication,

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:34.920
imprinted during
early childhood have

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:37.530
continued to serve me well.

00:18:37.530 --> 00:18:44.220
In any culture, there's simply
no substitute for hard work.

00:18:44.220 --> 00:18:46.500
I've been an
American for so long

00:18:46.500 --> 00:18:51.120
that I often forget that
I'm also an immigrant.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:54.290
From time to time, I could
still sense the desire

00:18:54.290 --> 00:18:58.440
that burns in the belly of
a new immigrant, the desire

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:02.160
to carve out a place
in the new world,

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:06.090
in the land of opportunities.

00:19:06.090 --> 00:19:11.130
To this day, I maintain
an underdog mentality

00:19:11.130 --> 00:19:16.970
that motivates me to a
higher level of work ethics.

00:19:16.970 --> 00:19:21.750
Let me begin to
close with a comment.

00:19:21.750 --> 00:19:23.880
Throughout its
history, Americans

00:19:23.880 --> 00:19:29.400
continue to benefit from the
drive labor and creativity

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:34.110
of immigrants, many in the field
of science and engineering.

00:19:34.110 --> 00:19:38.460
Just look among the
graduates this morning.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:44.340
Thus, today, one prevailing
view that immigrants constitute

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:49.410
a constant drain on our society
is simply baseless, wrong,

00:19:49.410 --> 00:19:53.650
and shameful, particularly
in this nation of immigrants.

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:56.125
[APPLAUSE]

00:20:03.550 --> 00:20:08.230
Graduates, as future
scientists and engineers,

00:20:08.230 --> 00:20:11.500
it is likely that you
will, on occasions,

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:15.550
be underappreciated,
under recognized,

00:20:15.550 --> 00:20:18.655
and more likely,
underpaid by our society.

00:20:18.655 --> 00:20:21.120
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:24.340
You will have to take
solace and satisfaction

00:20:24.340 --> 00:20:27.100
in knowing that
your work has helped

00:20:27.100 --> 00:20:31.750
to build a better, safer,
healthier, and richer world,

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:34.150
a world with hope.

00:20:34.150 --> 00:20:36.880
Members of the class
of '98, it matters

00:20:36.880 --> 00:20:42.910
not whether you work on numbers,
atoms, molecules, machines,

00:20:42.910 --> 00:20:46.540
organisms, the
Earth, or the cosmos.

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:50.620
Through science and
technology, any one of you

00:20:50.620 --> 00:20:54.370
can cast a giant
shadow on our planet.

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:57.370
Congratulations, and don't
forget to thank your parents.

00:20:57.370 --> 00:20:57.970
[LAUGHTER]

00:20:57.970 --> 00:21:00.720
[APPLAUSE]

